{{- /*
Xray config. Morzer renders it to <etc>/application.yaml, Compose mounts it as
/etc/xray/config.json, and ghcr.io/morzecrew/xray replaces each
{"$file": "<path>"} with that file's content, {"$vlessDecryption": "<path>"}
with the server key derived from that seed, and {"$vlessClients": "<path>"}
with the client list in that file, before starting Xray (platform-images#125,
#130). No secret value is ever in this file.

REALITY owns the public TLS port. A client holding the key gets Xray; anything
else (browsers, scanners, ACME, a CDN) is relayed to Caddy, which serves the
cover site with its own certificate and passes the XHTTP path back here.
Every REALITY inbound takes the PROXY header the gate opens each connection
with, naming the visitor, and opens what it relays with one of its own
(xver), so Caddy and the app behind it see the visitor, not Xray; the
second key's twins take it from the inbound that hands them on.

The xhttp-reality profiles add XHTTP under REALITY instead of TLS, on a port of
its own (8443 here, xhttp_reality_port outside): same keys, same fallback to
Caddy. No flow on it, as on any XHTTP inbound: Vision is for RAW only.

The mkcp profile adds VLESS over mKCP on UDP (8447 here, kcp_port outside):
no TLS underneath, so another protocol family for the network to block. Its
packets are AES-128-GCM under kcp_seed, then dressed in kcp_header; a client
must name both, in that order, as a link's seed and headerType do. With
kcp_open=quic a finalmask noise mask sends a QUIC-Initial-shaped packet before
the flow, so a network that lets QUIC out sees the opening as QUIC; the client
carries the whole mask list, this one included, in its link's fm=.

The hysteria2 profile adds Hysteria2, Xray's own, on UDP (8448 here,
hysteria2_port outside): QUIC with the site's own certificate, which Caddy
takes (read from its data, reloaded hourly), so it is another protocol family
again, and what the network sees is an HTTP/3 connection to this domain. Each
device's UUID is its login ({"$hysteriaUsers": "<path>"}, from the same
clients file), and a connection that brings none is answered by the cover
site over HTTP/3. Its congestion control is BBR, or Brutal at hysteria2_up
Mbit/s toward a device that asks for it.

There is no direct (freedom) outbound: if the upstream proxy is down, traffic
stops rather than leaving from this server's own IP. Nor could there be one:
this container sits on internal networks only, and its one way out is the
gate (gate.json.tmpl), which dials the upstream with the credentials this
container never holds. The last routing rule
names the upstream as the default outright, after the blocks, rather than
leaving it to Xray's rule that the first outbound is the default; UDP the
upstream cannot relay is blocked before it.
*/ -}}
{{- $domain := required "install with --domain" .Installation.Domain -}}
{{- /* resolve=gateway: names are resolved here, over DoH that itself goes out
through the upstream, and the upstream is handed addresses only, so it never
learns the hostnames. ForceIP, not UseIP: UseIP falls back to sending the name
when resolution fails. IPOnDemand resolves before the routing rules, so a name
that points at a private address is blocked like the address. resolve=upstream
hands the upstream the names, as before. */ -}}
{{- $gateway := eq .Parameters.resolve "gateway" -}}
{{- /* ipv6=separate: IPv6 goes its own way, through the upstreams lines marked
group=ipv6, which the gate reaches on its second hop (1081). A destination
with an IPv6 address leaves through them: with resolve=gateway, IPOnDemand
resolves a name for the ::/0 rule, so a name with an AAAA record matches it,
and the hop hands those lines IPv6 addresses only (ForceIPv6) and the first
hop IPv4 only (ForceIPv4). Neither falls back to the other. With
resolve=upstream only IPv6 literals match. IPv6 UDP is blocked: the hop to it
carries none. */ -}}
{{- $v6 := eq .Parameters.ipv6 "separate" -}}
{{- /* ipv6=block: no IPv6 destination leaves. DNS asks for A records only and
the upstreams are handed IPv4 addresses only (ForceIPv4), so a dual-stack name
still works over IPv4; what is left, IPv6 literals, the ::/0 rule blocks. With
resolve=upstream the upstream resolves names itself, so only literals stop. */ -}}
{{- $block6 := eq .Parameters.ipv6 "block" -}}
{{- /* UDP goes to the gate only when every upstreams line carries it, which
is the operator's to say: a WireGuard or OpenVPN line, or SOCKS5 with UDP
ASSOCIATE. */ -}}
{{- $udp := eq (print .Parameters.upstream_udp) "true" -}}
{{- /* reality_second_key: a second REALITY key beside reality_key, so a key can
be rotated with no device cut off. The public inbounds pass on to their
loopback twin under the second key what they can't authenticate, as they
pass a stranger on to the site; the twin passes its own strangers on to the
site, so a prober meets the site through both, and a device holding either
key gets in. */ -}}
{{- $second := eq (print .Parameters.reality_second_key) "true" -}}
{{- $withXdns := has "xdns" .Profiles -}}
{{- $xhttpReality := has "xhttp-reality" .Profiles -}}
{{- $kcp := has "mkcp" .Profiles -}}
{{- $hy2 := has "hysteria2" .Profiles -}}
{{- /* The two hosts exit-title asks (exit_ipv4_url, exit_info_url): plain
http:// URLs, each host and port forwarded to by a title- inbound below. */ -}}
{{- if ne (index (split "://" .Parameters.exit_ipv4_url) 0) "http" }}{{ required "exit_ipv4_url is a plain http:// URL" "" }}{{ end -}}
{{- if ne (index (split "://" .Parameters.exit_info_url) 0) "http" }}{{ required "exit_info_url is a plain http:// URL" "" }}{{ end -}}
{{- $ipHP := index (split "/" (replace .Parameters.exit_ipv4_url "http://" "")) 0 -}}
{{- $infoHP := index (split "/" (replace .Parameters.exit_info_url "http://" "")) 0 -}}
{{- /* The site's certificate as Caddy keeps it (caddy/Caddyfile): Let's
Encrypt's directory for acme and acme-dns, Caddy's own CA's for internal, and
with acme-dns the wildcard over the domain's parent (caddy.env.tmpl). */ -}}
{{- $labels := split "." $domain -}}
{{- $issuer := "acme-v02.api.letsencrypt.org-directory" -}}
{{- if eq .Parameters.cert_issuer "internal" }}{{ $issuer = "local" }}{{ end -}}
{{- $cert := $domain -}}
{{- if and (eq .Parameters.cert_issuer "acme-dns") (gt (len $labels) 2) }}{{ $cert = printf "wildcard_.%s" (join (slice $labels 1) ".") }}{{ end -}}
{{- if and $hy2 $kcp (eq .Parameters.hysteria2_port .Parameters.kcp_port) }}{{ required "hysteria2_port must differ from kcp_port" "" }}{{ end -}}
{{- $kcpOpen := and $kcp (eq .Parameters.kcp_open "quic") -}}
{{- $xdns := .Parameters.xdns_domain -}}
{{- if not (contains $xdns ".") }}{{ $xdns = printf "%s.%s" $xdns $domain }}{{ end -}}
{
  "log": {"loglevel": "{{ .Parameters.log_level }}", "access": "none", "dnsLog": false},
{{- $metrics := has "metrics" .Profiles -}}
{{- $counted := has "device-traffic" .Profiles -}}
{{- if $metrics }}
  {{- /* The metrics profile: traffic per inbound and outbound, never per
  device (its exporter leaves the user counters out), and the observatory's
  verdicts, under xray-metrics, a name this has on the metrics network alone,
  where the metrics profile's exporter reads them (compose/metrics.yaml,
  metrics/metrics.sh). */}}
  "metrics": {"listen": "xray-metrics:11111"},
{{- end }}
{{- if $counted }}
  {{- /* The device-traffic profile: the bytes each device moves, by its name,
  read by the facts task alone from the StatsService on loopback, which it
  reaches in this container's namespace (compose/device-traffic.yaml). A
  device's traffic to 127.0.0.1 never gets here: routing blocks private addresses. */}}
  "api": {"tag": "api", "listen": "127.0.0.1:10085", "services": ["StatsService"]},
{{- end }}
{{- if or $metrics $counted }}
  "stats": {},
  "policy": {
    {{- if $counted }}"levels": {"0": {"statsUserUplink": true, "statsUserDownlink": true}}{{ if $metrics }}, {{ end }}{{ end }}
    {{- if $metrics }}"system": {"statsInboundUplink": true, "statsInboundDownlink": true, "statsOutboundUplink": true, "statsOutboundDownlink": true}{{ end }}},
{{- end }}
{{- if $gateway }}
  {{- /* A name looked up before and since expired is answered from the cache
  at once (TTL 1) while it is asked again in the background, so a site
  revisited costs no round trip through the upstream to dns_resolver; for a
  day at most, after which it is asked first. */}}
  "dns": {"servers": ["{{ .Parameters.dns_resolver }}"], "queryStrategy": "{{ if $block6 }}UseIPv4{{ else }}UseIP{{ end }}", "disableFallback": true,
    "serveStale": true, "serveExpiredTTL": 86400, "tag": "dns"},
{{- end }}
  "inbounds": [
    {
      "tag": "reality",
      "listen": "0.0.0.0",
      "port": 443,
      "protocol": "vless",
      "settings": {
        "clients": {"$vlessClients": "/run/secrets/vless_clients"},
        "flow": "xtls-rprx-vision",
        "decryption": {"$vlessDecryption": "/run/secrets/vless_seed"}
      },
      "streamSettings": {
        "network": "raw",
        "security": "reality",
        "sockopt": {"acceptProxyProtocol": true},
        "realitySettings": {
          "xver": 1,
          "target": "{{ if $second }}127.0.0.1:4430{{ else }}caddy:443{{ end }}",
          "serverNames": ["{{ $domain }}"],
          "privateKey": {"$file": "/run/secrets/reality_key"},
          "shortIds": [{"$file": "/run/secrets/reality_short_id"}]
        }
      },
      "sniffing": {"enabled": true, "destOverride": ["http", "tls", "quic"], "routeOnly": true}
    },
{{- if $second }}
    {
      "tag": "reality-second",
      "listen": "127.0.0.1",
      "port": 4430,
      "protocol": "vless",
      "settings": {
        "clients": {"$vlessClients": "/run/secrets/vless_clients"},
        "flow": "xtls-rprx-vision",
        "decryption": {"$vlessDecryption": "/run/secrets/vless_seed"}
      },
      "streamSettings": {
        "network": "raw",
        "security": "reality",
        "sockopt": {"acceptProxyProtocol": true},
        "realitySettings": {
          "xver": 1,
          "target": "caddy:443",
          "serverNames": ["{{ $domain }}"],
          "privateKey": {"$file": "/run/secrets/reality_key_second"},
          "shortIds": [{"$file": "/run/secrets/reality_short_id_second"}]
        }
      },
      "sniffing": {"enabled": true, "destOverride": ["http", "tls", "quic"], "routeOnly": true}
    },
{{- end }}
    {
      "tag": "xhttp",
      "listen": "0.0.0.0",
      "port": 8080,
      "protocol": "vless",
      "settings": {
        "clients": {"$vlessClients": "/run/secrets/vless_clients"},
        "decryption": {"$vlessDecryption": "/run/secrets/vless_seed"}
      },
      "streamSettings": {
        "network": "xhttp",
        "xhttpSettings": {"path": {"$file": "/run/secrets/xhttp_path"}, "mode": "auto"}
      },
      "sniffing": {"enabled": true, "destOverride": ["http", "tls", "quic"], "routeOnly": true}
    },
{{- if $xhttpReality }}
    {
      "tag": "xhttp-reality",
      "listen": "0.0.0.0",
      "port": 8443,
      "protocol": "vless",
      "settings": {
        "clients": {"$vlessClients": "/run/secrets/vless_clients"},
        "decryption": {"$vlessDecryption": "/run/secrets/vless_seed"}
      },
      "streamSettings": {
        "network": "xhttp",
        "security": "reality",
        "sockopt": {"acceptProxyProtocol": true},
        "realitySettings": {
          "xver": 1,
          "target": "{{ if $second }}127.0.0.1:4431{{ else }}caddy:443{{ end }}",
          "serverNames": ["{{ $domain }}"],
          "privateKey": {"$file": "/run/secrets/reality_key"},
          "shortIds": [{"$file": "/run/secrets/reality_short_id"}]
        },
        "xhttpSettings": {"path": {"$file": "/run/secrets/xhttp_path"}, "mode": "auto"}
      },
      "sniffing": {"enabled": true, "destOverride": ["http", "tls", "quic"], "routeOnly": true}
    },
{{- if $second }}
    {
      "tag": "xhttp-reality-second",
      "listen": "127.0.0.1",
      "port": 4431,
      "protocol": "vless",
      "settings": {
        "clients": {"$vlessClients": "/run/secrets/vless_clients"},
        "decryption": {"$vlessDecryption": "/run/secrets/vless_seed"}
      },
      "streamSettings": {
        "network": "xhttp",
        "security": "reality",
        "sockopt": {"acceptProxyProtocol": true},
        "realitySettings": {
          "xver": 1,
          "target": "caddy:443",
          "serverNames": ["{{ $domain }}"],
          "privateKey": {"$file": "/run/secrets/reality_key_second"},
          "shortIds": [{"$file": "/run/secrets/reality_short_id_second"}]
        },
        "xhttpSettings": {"path": {"$file": "/run/secrets/xhttp_path"}, "mode": "auto"}
      },
      "sniffing": {"enabled": true, "destOverride": ["http", "tls", "quic"], "routeOnly": true}
    },
{{- end }}
{{- end }}
{{- if $kcp }}
    {
      "tag": "kcp",
      "listen": "0.0.0.0",
      "port": 8447,
      "protocol": "vless",
      "settings": {
        "clients": {"$vlessClients": "/run/secrets/vless_clients"},
        "decryption": {"$vlessDecryption": "/run/secrets/vless_seed"}
      },
      "streamSettings": {
        "network": "kcp",
        "finalmask": {"udp": [
          {"type": "mkcp-legacy", "settings": {"value": {"$file": "/run/secrets/kcp_seed"}}}
{{- if ne .Parameters.kcp_header "none" }},
          {"type": "mkcp-legacy", "settings": {"header": "{{ .Parameters.kcp_header }}"}}
{{- end }}
{{- if $kcpOpen }},
          {"type": "noise", "settings": {"noise": [{"type": "hex", "packet": "c300000001088394c8f03e5157080000449e00000002", "delay": "0-0"}]}}
{{- end }}
        ]}
      },
      "sniffing": {"enabled": true, "destOverride": ["http", "tls", "quic"], "routeOnly": true}
    },
{{- end }}
{{- if $hy2 }}
    {
      "tag": "hysteria2",
      "listen": "0.0.0.0",
      "port": 8448,
      "protocol": "hysteria",
      "settings": {"version": 2, "users": {"$hysteriaUsers": "/run/secrets/vless_clients"}},
      "streamSettings": {
        "network": "hysteria",
        "security": "tls",
        "tlsSettings": {"alpn": ["h3"], "certificates": [{
          "certificateFile": "/caddy-data/caddy/certificates/{{ $issuer }}/{{ $cert }}/{{ $cert }}.crt",
          "keyFile": "/caddy-data/caddy/certificates/{{ $issuer }}/{{ $cert }}/{{ $cert }}.key"}]},
        {{- /* The masquerade passes the visitor's own Host on to Caddy, as REALITY
        does: with rewriteHost, Xray's reverse proxy sent caddy:443 in its
        place, so the cover site's redirects and cookies over HTTP/3 named
        caddy where over TCP they name the domain. */}}
        "hysteriaSettings": {"version": 2, "udpIdleTimeout": 60,
          "masquerade": {"type": "proxy", "url": "https://caddy:443", "rewriteHost": false, "insecure": true}},
        {{- /* No stateless resets: Caddy's QUIC sends none, so a prober's stray
        packet to UDP 443 got an answer from Hysteria2 that the site's own
        HTTP/3 never gives (dotdash-lab #46). A device whose connection the
        server forgot times out instead.
        A connection silent for 15 s is given up: a device takes the lower of
        its own idle timeout and this one, and Xray's devices pin their own at
        Chrome's 30 s while they mimic its QUIC, so only this one shortens a
        frozen connection's 31 s to 16 (dotdash-lab #59). Devices' keepalives
        hold a healthy idle one open. */}}
        "finalmask": {"quicParams": {"disableStatelessReset": true, "maxIdleTimeout": 15, {{ if eq (printf "%v" .Parameters.hysteria2_up) "0" }}"congestion": "bbr"{{ else }}"congestion": "brutal", "brutalUp": "{{ .Parameters.hysteria2_up }} mbps"{{ end }}}}
      },
      "sniffing": {"enabled": true, "destOverride": ["http", "tls", "quic"], "routeOnly": true}
    },
{{- end }}
{{- if $withXdns }}
    {
      "tag": "xdns",
      "listen": "0.0.0.0",
      "port": 5353,
      "protocol": "vless",
      "settings": {
        "clients": {"$vlessClients": "/run/secrets/vless_clients"},
        "decryption": {"$vlessDecryption": "/run/secrets/vless_seed"}
      },
      "streamSettings": {
        "network": "kcp",
        "kcpSettings": {"mtu": {{ .Parameters.xdns_mtu }}, "tti": {{ .Parameters.xdns_tti }}},
        "finalmask": {"udp": [{"type": "xdns", "settings": {"domains": [{"names": ["{{ $xdns }}"], "edns0": 1232}]}}]}
      },
      "sniffing": {"enabled": true, "destOverride": ["http", "tls", "quic"], "routeOnly": true}
    },
{{- end }}
{{- if has "openflux" .Profiles }}
    {{- /* The openflux profile's exits' way out (openflux/openflux.sh): their
    connections take the routing devices' do, the blocks and resolve and
    ipv6 included, then the upstream. On the flux network, which only
    openflux shares with Xray, beside Xray's other internal networks; UDP as
    upstream_udp says, the hop refusing it otherwise. */}}
    {
      "tag": "openflux",
      "listen": "0.0.0.0",
      "port": 1082,
      "protocol": "socks",
      "settings": {"auth": "noauth", "udp": {{ $udp }}},
      "sniffing": {"enabled": true, "destOverride": ["http", "tls", "quic"], "routeOnly": true}
    },
{{- end }}
    {
      "tag": "probe",
      "listen": "127.0.0.1",
      "port": 10809,
      "protocol": "http"
    },
    {{- /* The exit-title service's way to the two hosts it asks, through the
    upstream as client traffic leaves, on the title network it shares with
    this container alone (compose.yaml): by name, so a re-created Xray takes
    nothing from it, where one in this namespace was left in the old one. Each
    forwards to one fixed host and nowhere else, so what else reaches the port
    (Caddy, the gate) can ask those two hosts and nothing more. */}}
    {
      "tag": "title-ip",
      "listen": "0.0.0.0",
      "port": 10812,
      "protocol": "dokodemo-door",
      "settings": {"address": "{{ index (split ":" $ipHP) 0 }}", "port": {{ if contains $ipHP ":" }}{{ index (split ":" $ipHP) 1 }}{{ else }}80{{ end }}, "network": "tcp"}
    },
    {
      "tag": "title-info",
      "listen": "0.0.0.0",
      "port": 10813,
      "protocol": "dokodemo-door",
      "settings": {"address": "{{ index (split ":" $infoHP) 0 }}", "port": {{ if contains $infoHP ":" }}{{ index (split ":" $infoHP) 1 }}{{ else }}80{{ end }}, "network": "tcp"}
    }
  ],
  "outbounds": [
    {"tag": "upstream", "protocol": "socks", "settings": {"address": "gate", "port": 1080}{{ if $gateway }}, "targetStrategy": "{{ if or $v6 $block6 }}ForceIPv4{{ else }}ForceIPv4v6{{ end }}"{{ end }}},
    {"tag": "block", "protocol": "blackhole"}
{{- if $v6 }},
    {"tag": "upstream6", "protocol": "socks", "settings": {"address": "gate", "port": 1081}{{ if $gateway }}, "targetStrategy": "ForceIPv6"{{ end }}}
{{- end }}
  ],
  "routing": {
    "domainStrategy": "{{ if $gateway }}IPOnDemand{{ else }}AsIs{{ end }}",
    "rules": [
{{- if $gateway }}
      {"inboundTag": ["dns"], "outboundTag": "upstream"},
{{- end }}
      {"ip": ["geoip:private"], "outboundTag": "block"},
      {{- /* What the upstreams' providers forbid. Sniffing sees a plain
      BitTorrent handshake alone, so the announces that put the exit address
      in public swarms go by the trackers' names; client UDP, DHT's way, is
      dropped below unless upstream_udp. Mail is sent on 465 and 587, never
      by a device on 25. */}}
      {"protocol": ["bittorrent"], "outboundTag": "block"},
      {"domain": ["geosite:category-public-tracker"], "outboundTag": "block"},
      {"network": "tcp", "port": "25", "outboundTag": "block"}
      {{- if not $udp }},
      {"network": "udp", "outboundTag": "block"}
      {{- end }},
{{- if $v6 }}
      {"network": "udp", "ip": ["::/0"], "outboundTag": "block"},
      {"ip": ["::/0"], "outboundTag": "upstream6"},
{{- end }}
{{- if $block6 }}
      {"ip": ["::/0"], "outboundTag": "block"},
{{- end }}
      {"network": "tcp,udp", "outboundTag": "upstream"}
    ]
  }
}
